# Data Sufficiency in Reasoning Problems

> Reasoning DS replaces arithmetic with arrangements: seats, relations,
> codes and ranks. A statement suffices when the clues leave exactly one
> possibility standing — this lesson shows how many fixed seats, symbols
> or anchor pairs that takes, topic by topic.

[important]
In reasoning DS you are not computing a number — you are counting
arrangements. Build every possibility a statement allows; the moment
more than one survives, that statement (or combination) is insufficient.
[/important]

## 1. How Reasoning DS Differs

@diagram tree id="reasoning-ds-tree" layout="tree" aria="Reasoning data sufficiency topic families"
  "Reasoning DS" -> "Seating arrangements"
  "Reasoning DS" -> "Blood relations"
  "Reasoning DS" -> "Coding-decoding"
  "Reasoning DS" -> "Ordering and ranking"
  "Reasoning DS" -> "Direction sense"
  "Seating arrangements" -> "Rows"
  "Seating arrangements" -> "Circles"
@/diagram

The stem hands you a puzzle with a question mark — who is opposite C,
how is M related to K, what is the code for "blue". The statements hand
you clues. The verdict measures *constraint*, not cleverness:

| Arithmetic DS | Reasoning DS |
| --- | --- |
| unknowns are numbers | unknowns are positions, links or labels |
| check: equations vs unknowns | check: arrangements vs uniqueness |
| one equation, two unknowns = insufficient | one relative clue, no anchor = insufficient |
| combine = solve the system | combine = merge the clue set and rebuild |

## 2. The Uniqueness Test

[definition]
A clue set is **sufficient** exactly when it admits one and only one
answer to the stem. Two valid arrangements that disagree on the asked
seat, code or relation mean the set is insufficient — even if both
arrangements agree on everything *else*.
[/definition]

@diagram flowchart id="uniqueness-flow" layout="hierarchical" aria="Uniqueness test flow for reasoning data sufficiency"
node start label="Read the stem: which seat, code or relation is asked?" shape="pill"
node b1 label="Build the arrangement from statement I" shape="round-rect"
node u1 label="Exactly one possibility survives?" shape="diamond"
node b2 label="Rebuild using statement II alone" shape="round-rect"
node u2 label="Exactly one possibility survives?" shape="diamond"
node bc label="Merge I and II and rebuild" shape="round-rect"
node uc label="Exactly one possibility survives?" shape="diamond"
node out label="Mark the matching option" shape="round-rect"

start -> b1
b1 -> u1
u1 -> out
u1 -> b2
b2 -> u2
u2 -> out
u2 -> bc
bc -> uc
uc -> out
@/diagram

[tip]
Draw before you decide. A five-seat row or a six-seat circle takes ten
seconds to sketch, and every surviving arrangement becomes visible.
Verdicts announced from memory of the words alone are the ones that go
wrong.
[/tip]

## 3. Seating Arrangements: How Many Fixed Seats Are Enough

A row clue is "fixed" when it pins a person to one seat (extreme left,
middle, second from right). Relative clues (to the left of, between)
only pin seats when an anchor exists. Sufficiency arrives when the asked
seat is fixed.

**Example 1 — neither suffices.** Five friends A, B, C, D, E sit in a row
facing north. Who sits in the middle?
- **I.** A is at the extreme left and E is at the extreme right.
- **II.** B is immediately to the left of E, and C sits between A and B.

I alone: A _ _ _ E — the middle is open. II alone: B lands at seat 4,
but C between A (seat 1) and B (seat 4) can sit at seat 2 *or* seat 3.
Two arrangements survive in each case — and even merged:

```text
A C D B E   -> middle is D
A D C B E   -> middle is C
```

Both fit every clue and disagree on the answer. Verdict: **neither is
sufficient even together**.

**Example 2 — both together.** Five people P, Q, R, S, T sit in a row
facing north. Who sits second from the right?
- **I.** T is at the extreme left and Q is at the extreme right.
- **II.** S sits exactly in the middle, and P is immediately to the left of S.

I alone: T _ _ _ Q — insufficient. II alone: P _ S _ _ — three people
still float. Merged: T P S R Q, so seat 4 (second from right) is **P** —
one arrangement survives. Verdict: **both together**.

**Example 3 — either alone, and rotation does not matter.** Six persons
A–F sit around a circular table facing the centre. Who sits opposite C?
- **I.** A sits opposite D, and B sits opposite E.
- **II.** E sits third to the right of C.

I alone: two opposite pairs are set, so the remaining pair (C, F) must
face each other — sufficient. II alone: in a six-seat circle, third to
the right is *directly opposite*, so C faces E — sufficient. Verdict:
**either alone**. Notice the circle may be rotated freely; who sits
opposite whom survives rotation.

[warning]
Relative seats need anchors. "C sits between A and B" fixes nothing
unless the gap is one seat or an anchor fixes one end. A chain of
relative clues with no extreme or numbered seat can survive forever and
still be insufficient.
[/warning]

## 4. Blood Relations and Symbol Notation

Relation puzzles pin two things: the *link* (parent, sibling, child) and
the *gender*. Symbol notation compresses the links; the gender comes
only from the symbols — never from the names.

| Symbol | Meaning | Sufficiency value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A + B | A is male | needed to say father, uncle, son |
| A − B | A is female | needed to say mother, aunt, daughter |
| A → B | A is the parent of B | the core link |
| A ↔ B | A and B are a married couple | links two families |

**Example 1 — I alone.** How is M related to K?
- **I.** K is the sister of L, and M is the mother of L.
- **II.** M has exactly two children, K and L.

I alone: M is L's mother and K is L's sister, so M is K's mother too —
link and gender both pinned. Sufficient. II alone: M is K's *parent*,
but M's gender is unstated — mother or father both survive.
Verdict: **I alone**.

**Example 2 — II alone.** How is A related to B?
- **I.** B is the son of C, and C is the sister of A.
- **II.** A is the brother of C, and B is the son of C.

I alone: A is C's sibling but A's gender is unknown — uncle *or* aunt.
Insufficient. II alone: A is male and C's sibling, B is C's son, so A is
B's uncle. Sufficient. Verdict: **II alone**.

[note]
A relation that ends in "uncle or aunt" or "son or daughter" is the
signature of a missing gender symbol. If the asked relation has a
gendered word, some statement must mark gender — or the verdict is
insufficient.
[/note]

## 5. Coding and Decoding

In word-to-code questions, a sentence pair works like an equation set:
shared words take shared codes, and a code becomes *named* only when
every other word in the sentence is accounted for.

**Example 1 — both together.** What is the code for "blue"?
- **I.** "sky is blue" is written as "pa qe ri".
- **II.** "blue ink dries" is written as "ri ta ve".

I alone: blue is one of pa/qe/ri — unnameable. II alone: same problem.
Merged: "blue" is the only word common to both sentences and "ri" is
the only code common to both, so blue = ri. Verdict: **both together**.

**Example 2 — I alone, using an internal anchor.** What is the code for
"rain"?
- **I.** "rain falls here" is written as "do re me", and "snow falls here" is written as "do re fa".
- **II.** "heavy rain came" is written as "me so la".

I alone contains *two* sentences: shared words "falls here" take the
shared codes "do re", leaving "me" unmatched in the first and "fa"
unmatched in the second — so rain = me. One statement, fully sufficient.
II alone has three words against three codes and no anchor. Verdict:
**I alone**.

[tip]
Count unmatched words against unmatched codes. A code is named the
moment the sentence has exactly one unexplained word — that is what
statement II achieves in Example 2 and what merging achieves in
Example 1.
[/tip]

## 6. Ordering and Ranking

Rank questions convert freely between ends: position from the top =
total − position from the bottom + 1. Sufficiency needs either both ends
named, or one end and the total.

**Example 1 — either alone.** What is Ravi's rank from the top in a class
of 40?
- **I.** Ravi is 23rd from the bottom.
- **II.** Ravi is 8 ranks below Sunil, and Sunil is 10th from the top.

I alone: 40 − 23 + 1 = 18th — sufficient. II alone: 10 + 8 = 18th —
sufficient. Verdict: **either alone**.

**Example 2 — both together, with the order fixed.** How many students
are in the row?
- **I.** A is 15th from the left end.
- **II.** B is 20th from the right end, lies to the right of A, with exactly 6 students between them.

I alone: no right-end data. II alone: A's position floats. Merged:
A at 15, six students after, so B at 22 from the left; B is 20th from
the right, so total = 22 + 20 − 1 = 41. Verdict: **both together**.

[warning]
Where a "between" count appears without the *order* of the two people,
the total has two possible values. If statement II had not said that B
lies to the right of A, the merged data would allow two totals — and
the verdict would drop to neither.
[/warning]

## 7. Direction Sense

Direction DS is geometry without numbers: a chain of relative bearings
fixes the asked direction whenever every step is axis-aligned.

**Example — either alone.** In which direction is B with respect to A?
- **I.** B is north of C, and C is east of A.
- **II.** B is 4 km north of D, and D is 3 km north of A.

I alone: go east from A to reach C, then north to reach B — B is
north-east of A, whatever the distances. Sufficient. II alone: the path
A → D → B runs due north twice — B is north of A. Sufficient. Verdict:
**either alone**.

[info]
Distances matter only when the stem asks for *distance* (or the path
bends). For a pure direction stem, the bearings alone decide — a
statement full of kilometres can still be sufficient or insufficient
for reasons that have nothing to do with the numbers.
[/info]

## 8. Traps and Timing

A well-set reasoning DS section also spreads its verdicts, with
"neither" appearing more often than in arithmetic because broken clue
chains are easy to plant:

@chart donut title="Typical answer spread in a 40-question reasoning DS set (illustrative)" labels:
I alone
II alone
Either alone
Both together
Neither

data:
9
9
8
10
4
@/chart

| Trap | What it looks like | Defence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| First-fit arrest | stopping at the first arrangement that fits | enumerate *all* survivors before judging |
| Gender from names | assuming Meena must be female | gender exists only where a symbol states it |
| Relative without anchor | "B is to the left of C" in a free row | demand an extreme, middle or numbered seat |
| Rotated circle panic | circle drawing looks different each try | rotate to a canonical start; opposite pairs survive |
| "Between" ambiguity | two people fit "between A and B" | check the gap size, not the phrase |
| Unanchored sentence pair | three words, three codes, no overlap | a code is nameable only with a shared anchor |

[tip]
Budget **30–45 seconds per question**. Spend the first five writing the
frame — five blanks for a row, six dots for a circle, two columns for a
code pair — then place clues and count survivors. If a statement leaves
a two-case fork, that fork *is* the verdict: insufficient.
[/tip]

[warning]
Do not mark "both together" the moment merging *reduces* the
possibilities. Reduction is not uniqueness — a clue set that cuts six
arrangements to two has improved but still failed the stem.
[/warning]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Topic | What makes a statement sufficient | Common verdict |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Seating (rows) | asked seat pinned; all others irrelevant | both together |
| Seating (circles) | opposite pairs or a counted offset | either or both |
| Blood relations | link plus gender both pinned | depends on symbols |
| Coding-decoding | one unmatched word against one unmatched code | both together |
| Ordering and ranking | two ends, or one end plus the total | either |
| Direction sense | axis-aligned chain to the asked bearing | either |

| Point | One-line recap |
| --- | --- |
| Master rule | exactly one surviving arrangement = sufficient |
| Test order | I alone, then II alone, then merge |
| Gender rule | relations stay ambiguous without a gender symbol |
| Code rule | shared word pairs name codes; orphans do not |
| Rank rule | top = total − bottom + 1 |
| Time budget | 30–45 seconds, drawn not memorised |

[summary]
Reasoning DS is a uniqueness game played on paper. Sketch the frame,
lay each statement's clues on it, and count the arrangements that
survive the stem's question. Rows need anchors, relations need gender,
codes need shared pairs, ranks need two ends — and a verdict is earned
only when one possibility is left standing.
[/summary]
